<file path="docs/tests/edge-readability.html">
<!-- Edge Case: Readability & Typography -->
<!-- Tests: heading density, small text, Coleman-Liau, paragraph length, lists, letter spacing, paragraph spacing, visual hierarchy -->
<html lang="en">
<head><meta charset="UTF-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
<style>body{font-family:system-ui;margin:0;padding:24px;line-height:1.6}.section{margin-bottom:32px;padding:16px;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;border-radius:8px}</style>
</head><body>
<main>

<section class="section">
  <h1>Edge Case: Readability</h1>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <h2>1. Long paragraph (> 150 words)</h2>
  <p>This is an intentionally long paragraph designed to test the paragraph length checker. It contains many sentences that go on and on, covering multiple topics without any natural breaks. The reader must track through all of this text without the benefit of subheadings, bullet points, or any other visual chunking mechanism. Research from the Nielsen Norman Group shows that users scan web content rather than reading it word-by-word. When paragraphs exceed 150 words, they become walls of text that most users will skip entirely. The ideal paragraph length for web content is 40-80 words. By breaking content into smaller chunks and using descriptive subheadings, you can improve scanability by up to 47%. This paragraph deliberately exceeds the 150-word threshold to verify that the analyzer correctly flags it. It keeps going to make absolutely sure we cross that boundary with plenty of margin. Additional sentences are added here to pad the word count even further beyond what would be considered good practice for web writing.</p>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <h2>2. Complex vocabulary (high Coleman-Liau grade)</h2>
  <p>The anthropomorphization of computational algorithms demonstrates an epistemological predisposition toward reductionist interpretations of neurobiological phenomena. Notwithstanding the aforementioned methodological constraints, the interdisciplinary synthesis of psycholinguistic and neurocognitive paradigms necessitates a comprehensive reconceptualization of established theoretical frameworks.</p>
  <p>Furthermore, the juxtaposition of phenomenological and empiricist perspectives illuminates the inherent contradictions within contemporary epistemological discourse, thereby undermining the foundational assumptions of positivist methodologies.</p>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <h2>3. No content lists (wall of text)</h2>
  <p>Feature one is great because it does something useful. Feature two is also important for different reasons. Feature three helps with a specific problem. Feature four addresses another need entirely. These should really be in a list for better scanability.</p>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <h2>4. With proper lists</h2>
  <p>Our key features include:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Feature one — does something useful</li>
    <li>Feature two — important for different reasons</li>
    <li>Feature three — helps with a specific problem</li>
    <li>Feature four — addresses another need</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <h2>5. Very small text</h2>
  <p style="font-size:9px">This text is only 9px — nearly impossible to read</p>
  <p style="font-size:10px">This is 10px — too small for most users</p>
  <p style="font-size:11px">This is 11px — still below the 12px minimum</p>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <h2>6. Extreme letter spacing</h2>
  <p style="letter-spacing: -0.05em;">Extremely tight letter spacing (-0.05em) — hard to read</p>
  <p style="letter-spacing: 0.3em;">Extremely loose letter spacing (0.3em) — hard to read</p>
  <p style="letter-spacing: -0.01em;">Acceptable negative spacing (-0.01em)</p>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <h2>7. Inconsistent paragraph spacing</h2>
  <p style="margin-bottom: 4px;">Very tight paragraph spacing (4px)</p>
  <p style="margin-bottom: 4px;">Second paragraph with tight spacing</p>
  <p style="margin-bottom: 4px;">Third paragraph with tight spacing</p>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <h2>8. Good readability (control)</h2>
  <p>This paragraph has good readability. It uses clear, simple language at a comfortable reading level. Sentences are short and direct. The font size is 16px with proper line height.</p>
  <p>A second paragraph with adequate spacing shows proper paragraph breaks. The content flows naturally and is easy to scan.</p>
</section>

</main>
